WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.195
Lesson 4.8.

00:00:02.195 --> 00:00:05.340
Tokenized treasuries and real-world assets (RWAs).

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.607
By the end, you'll know exactly what you

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:10.870
actually own, and specifically who you're trusting, before

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.695
ever holding one of these tokens.

00:00:13.995 --> 00:00:15.285
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.285 --> 00:00:19.380
What an R.W.A. token actually represents, off-chain.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.960
Exactly who you're trusting, across four separate parties.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.083
Access limits, and composability, the two practical constraints

00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:29.895
that decide how usable one actually is.

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:32.549
And a full worked example, using the source

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:35.927
material's own numbers for a tokenized treasury product.

00:00:37.227 --> 00:00:39.552
What an RWA actually represents.

00:00:40.852 --> 00:00:44.477
Here's what a real-world asset token genuinely is: a

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:49.017
token that represents an asset that exists entirely off-chain.

00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:52.617
Most commonly, that's short-term government bills, the

00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:55.077
single most common R.W.A. by far.

00:00:55.077 --> 00:00:57.147
But it can also be a money-market

00:00:57.147 --> 00:01:00.612
fund, private credit, commodities, or even property.

00:01:00.612 --> 00:01:03.937
The token itself is a claim, a representation.

00:01:03.937 --> 00:01:06.522
The actual asset it represents sits, and

00:01:06.522 --> 00:01:09.297
is managed, entirely outside the blockchain.

00:01:10.597 --> 00:01:13.141
Worth being precise about how a tokenized

00:01:13.141 --> 00:01:16.351
treasury product's yield typically works, since it's

00:01:16.351 --> 00:01:19.377
a genuinely simple, transparent relationship.

00:01:19.377 --> 00:01:22.273
It tracks the short-term government rate directly,

00:01:22.273 --> 00:01:24.787
minus the product's own management fee.

00:01:24.787 --> 00:01:27.931
There's no separate mechanism, no hidden formula,

00:01:27.931 --> 00:01:30.571
and importantly, none of the emissions or

00:01:30.571 --> 00:01:33.149
funding-rate risk that some of the other

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:36.167
stablecoin yield sources from last lesson carry.

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:40.445
Worth connecting this directly back to the four

00:01:40.445 --> 00:01:43.610
stablecoin yield sources from last lesson, since a

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:47.457
tokenized treasury is essentially that lesson's third source,

00:01:47.457 --> 00:01:50.797
treasury-backed, in its purest, most direct form.

00:01:50.797 --> 00:01:53.807
Its risk profile is genuinely different from the

00:01:53.807 --> 00:01:56.633
other three: no protocol code to worry about,

00:01:56.633 --> 00:01:59.337
no governance vote resetting a rate, and no

00:01:59.337 --> 00:02:02.347
funding payments that can silently turn negative.

00:02:03.647 --> 00:02:04.737
Quick check.

00:02:04.737 --> 00:02:08.072
What does a tokenized treasury product's yield actually track?

00:02:12.492 --> 00:02:15.420
The answer: short-term government rates, minus

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.911
whatever fee the product itself charges.

00:02:19.211 --> 00:02:20.566
Who you're actually trusting.

00:02:21.866 --> 00:02:24.267
Here's all four parties this actually runs

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:26.501
through, since the token itself is only

00:02:26.501 --> 00:02:28.421
as good as every one of them.

00:02:28.421 --> 00:02:31.285
The issuer is the legal structure that actually

00:02:31.285 --> 00:02:33.971
created the token, and stands behind it.

00:02:33.971 --> 00:02:36.728
The custodian is who physically, or legally,

00:02:36.728 --> 00:02:39.663
holds the real off-chain assets backing it.

00:02:39.663 --> 00:02:42.893
The redemption process is how, and how quickly, you

00:02:42.893 --> 00:02:46.423
can actually convert the token back into real value.

00:02:46.423 --> 00:02:49.285
And transfer rules decide where, and to whom,

00:02:49.285 --> 00:02:51.400
the token is even allowed to move.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.963
Worth being precise about why all four

00:02:54.963 --> 00:02:56.703
have to hold up, not just any

00:02:56.703 --> 00:02:58.385
one or two of them, for this

00:02:58.385 --> 00:03:00.890
to actually work the way you'd expect.

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:02.576
A sound issuer with a weak

00:03:02.576 --> 00:03:05.000
custodian is still a real problem.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.318
A sound issuer and custodian with a

00:03:07.318 --> 00:03:10.665
redemption process that quietly stalls under stress

00:03:10.665 --> 00:03:12.510
is still a real problem.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.478
Each one is a genuinely separate point of trust,

00:03:15.478 --> 00:03:17.900
not a repeated version of the same risk.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.602
Here's how to actually verify these four

00:03:21.602 --> 00:03:24.180
parties yourself, rather than just taking a

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.355
name on a website at face value.

00:03:26.355 --> 00:03:29.032
Find the issuer's actual legal structure

00:03:29.032 --> 00:03:32.400
and jurisdiction, documented, not just implied.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.774
Find independent evidence of what the custodian actually holds,

00:03:36.774 --> 00:03:40.765
ideally a real, regular attestation, not just a claim.

00:03:40.765 --> 00:03:44.002
And read the redemption terms directly, specifically

00:03:44.002 --> 00:03:47.055
for timing, minimums, and any conditions attached.

00:03:48.355 --> 00:03:49.445
Quick check.

00:03:49.445 --> 00:03:51.765
Name two parties you're actually

00:03:51.765 --> 00:03:53.875
trusting with an R.W.A. token.

00:03:58.175 --> 00:04:00.603
The answer: any two of the issuer, the

00:04:00.603 --> 00:04:04.525
custodian, the redemption agent, or the token's administrators.

00:04:05.825 --> 00:04:07.970
Access limits, and composability.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:13.215
Here's exactly what these two constraints each actually decide.

00:04:13.215 --> 00:04:16.923
Access limits decide who's even allowed to hold the

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:21.129
token in the first place, often restricted specifically to

00:04:21.129 --> 00:04:25.978
verified, K.Y.C.'d holders, or to accredited or qualified investors

00:04:25.978 --> 00:04:29.700
only, and not available at all in every country.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.314
Composability decides what you can actually do with

00:04:33.314 --> 00:04:36.094
it once you hold it: some R.W.A. tokens

00:04:36.094 --> 00:04:39.291
can be used freely as collateral elsewhere in

00:04:39.291 --> 00:04:42.627
DeFi, while others are restricted entirely to a

00:04:42.627 --> 00:04:45.337
specific allowlist of approved wallets.

00:04:46.638 --> 00:04:49.352
Worth being completely direct about the actual

00:04:49.352 --> 00:04:52.423
rule here, since it's simple and non-negotiable.

00:04:52.423 --> 00:04:55.107
Check your own eligibility, in your own actual

00:04:55.107 --> 00:04:58.376
country, before ever touching one of these products.

00:04:58.376 --> 00:05:02.048
Never attempt to work around a restriction that specifically

00:05:02.048 --> 00:05:05.118
applies to you; that's a legal and structural risk

00:05:05.118 --> 00:05:09.091
entirely separate from anything about the underlying asset itself.

00:05:10.391 --> 00:05:14.711
Here's what that composability difference actually looks like

00:05:14.711 --> 00:05:18.614
in practice, concretely, between two R.W.A. tokens that

00:05:18.614 --> 00:05:21.701
might otherwise look identical on paper.

00:05:21.701 --> 00:05:24.748
One with open composability can be deposited

00:05:24.748 --> 00:05:27.862
directly as collateral in an ordinary lending

00:05:27.862 --> 00:05:31.006
market, exactly like any other DeFi asset.

00:05:31.006 --> 00:05:33.919
One restricted to an allowlist can only move

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:37.480
between pre-approved wallets, meaning it can't be used

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:40.716
in most DeFi protocols at all, however attractive

00:05:40.716 --> 00:05:43.241
its own underlying yield happens to be.

00:05:44.541 --> 00:05:45.631
Quick check.

00:05:45.631 --> 00:05:47.802
Why does it actually matter to

00:05:47.802 --> 00:05:51.023
check an R.W.A. token's transfer restrictions?

00:05:55.443 --> 00:05:58.880
The answer: some R.W.A. tokens only move between

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.018
allowlisted wallets, which directly limits your ability to

00:06:03.018 --> 00:06:05.613
exit, or to use it elsewhere in DeFi.

00:06:06.913 --> 00:06:07.918
Worked example.

00:06:09.218 --> 00:06:11.650
Here's the calculation, exactly as the

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:14.693
source material lays it out, illustratively.

00:06:14.693 --> 00:06:18.778
Short-term government bills are yielding four point zero percent.

00:06:18.778 --> 00:06:21.524
The tokenized product charges a zero point

00:06:21.524 --> 00:06:24.015
one five percent management fee on top

00:06:24.015 --> 00:06:25.848
of holding them for you.

00:06:25.848 --> 00:06:28.578
Subtract that fee from the underlying rate, and

00:06:28.578 --> 00:06:31.821
you'd earn approximately three point eight five percent,

00:06:31.821 --> 00:06:34.608
before any additional on-chain costs of your own.

00:06:35.908 --> 00:06:38.575
Worth being precise about where this specific

00:06:38.575 --> 00:06:41.301
product could actually fit, using the own-bank

00:06:41.301 --> 00:06:43.688
ladder framework from Module twelve.

00:06:43.688 --> 00:06:46.307
It can genuinely suit the safer, near-term

00:06:46.307 --> 00:06:48.500
tiers of that ladder, but only, and

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:51.790
specifically, if its redemption time actually matches

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:54.648
when you'd genuinely need that money back.

00:06:54.648 --> 00:06:57.281
A product yielding an attractive rate is no

00:06:57.281 --> 00:06:59.735
use at all if your own redemption window

00:06:59.735 --> 00:07:02.488
doesn't line up with your own actual timeline.

00:07:03.788 --> 00:07:06.434
Here's that exact same point, made concrete,

00:07:06.434 --> 00:07:09.139
comparing a redemption time that matches your

00:07:09.139 --> 00:07:11.438
own need against one that doesn't.

00:07:11.438 --> 00:07:14.216
A redemption window shorter than, or equal to,

00:07:14.216 --> 00:07:16.994
when you'll actually need the money keeps this

00:07:16.994 --> 00:07:19.776
product genuinely usable for that purpose.

00:07:19.776 --> 00:07:22.998
A redemption window longer than your own timeline means

00:07:22.998 --> 00:07:25.588
you could be forced to sell into a secondary

00:07:25.588 --> 00:07:28.867
market instead, at whatever price that happens to offer,

00:07:28.867 --> 00:07:30.651
right when you needed the cash.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:35.383
Here's why this particular risk shape feels different from

00:07:35.383 --> 00:07:38.000
most of what this module has covered so far,

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.976
worth naming explicitly before closing it out.

00:07:40.976 --> 00:07:43.788
A vault, a restaking stack, or a synthetic

00:07:43.788 --> 00:07:47.973
funding position all carry genuinely technical, on-chain risks,

00:07:47.973 --> 00:07:52.196
code, oracles, correlated failure, funding turning negative.

00:07:52.196 --> 00:07:56.230
An R.W.A. token's risk is almost entirely off-chain and

00:07:56.230 --> 00:08:00.407
legal instead: who the issuer and custodian actually are,

00:08:00.407 --> 00:08:02.856
and whether they do what they say.

00:08:04.156 --> 00:08:05.616
Here's your checklist.

00:08:05.616 --> 00:08:08.901
Do it before holding any R.W.A. token.

00:08:08.901 --> 00:08:12.123
Confirm you're actually eligible in your own country, and

00:08:12.123 --> 00:08:14.811
that holding it is genuinely legal for you.

00:08:14.811 --> 00:08:17.328
Read the issuer, the custodian, and the

00:08:17.328 --> 00:08:20.349
redemption terms, all three, not just whichever

00:08:20.349 --> 00:08:22.096
one is easiest to find.

00:08:22.096 --> 00:08:25.996
Know its transfer restrictions and any minimum holding amounts.

00:08:25.996 --> 00:08:29.201
And size the entire position within caps, the exact

00:08:29.201 --> 00:08:32.221
same way you'd size any other single-issuer risk.

00:08:33.521 --> 00:08:35.636
What this whole module actually built.

00:08:36.936 --> 00:08:39.778
Here's the one skill this entire module has

00:08:39.778 --> 00:08:42.813
been building, across every lesson in it, from

00:08:42.813 --> 00:08:46.752
farming, through staking and restaking, to vaults, airdrops,

00:08:46.752 --> 00:08:49.571
stablecoins, and now real-world assets.

00:08:49.571 --> 00:08:52.918
Split any advertised yield into what it's genuinely earned

00:08:52.918 --> 00:08:55.941
from, and name the specific risk being paid for.

00:08:55.941 --> 00:08:58.599
That's the actual outcome this module set out

00:08:58.599 --> 00:09:01.026
to teach, back at its own Mastery Starter.

00:09:02.326 --> 00:09:03.571
Let's recap.

00:09:03.571 --> 00:09:06.501
An R.W.A. token represents an off-chain

00:09:06.501 --> 00:09:10.391
asset, most commonly short-term government bills.

00:09:10.391 --> 00:09:13.608
You're trusting four separate parties: the issuer, the

00:09:13.608 --> 00:09:17.301
custodian, the redemption process, and its transfer rules.

00:09:17.301 --> 00:09:20.477
Access limits and composability decide who can hold it,

00:09:20.477 --> 00:09:22.876
and what you can actually do with it.

00:09:22.876 --> 00:09:26.221
And the source material's own example shows the math

00:09:26.221 --> 00:09:29.188
is simple, four percent minus a small fee; the

00:09:29.188 --> 00:09:32.218
real work is verifying the trust behind it, and

00:09:32.218 --> 00:09:35.626
matching its redemption time to your own actual needs.

00:09:36.926 --> 00:09:39.791
Confirm your own eligibility, read all three

00:09:39.791 --> 00:09:42.782
of issuer, custodian and redemption terms, and

00:09:42.782 --> 00:09:45.756
match redemption timing to your own needs.

00:09:45.756 --> 00:09:47.681
That's the end of Module 4.

00:09:47.681 --> 00:09:49.236
Next up, Module 5.
